## Vanishing Gradient

The vanishing gradient problem happens when gradients become extremely small during backpropagation. As they move backward through many layers, the updates to the early layers become almost zero.

Result: The network learns very slowly (or stops learning) in the earlier layers, making it difficult to train deep neural networks.


## Exploding Gradient

The exploding gradient problem happens when gradients become extremely large during backpropagation. This causes very large weight updates, making the model unstable.

Result: The loss may fluctuate wildly, become very large, or even turn into NaN, preventing the network from learning properly.
